---
/**
 * The distribution of answers to one claim.
 *
 * A histogram, a median, and — since 2026-08-21 — a mean, in that order of prominence and for
 * that reason. The mean of an 11-point bipolar scale is misleading exactly when the
 * distribution is bimodal, and bimodal is what to expect on the contested claims: a single
 * number reports "6.2, mild agreement" for a community that has split cleanly into two camps.
 * The bars are the finding; the median is a summary of them that cannot invent a middle nobody
 * occupies; the mean is given because people ask for it.
 *
 * **The display rule is what carries the old prohibition, so it lives here rather than only in
 * CLAUDE.md.** The mean never appears as a headline, never on a card, never on a sort control,
 * and never without the histogram beside it. In this component that means: in the statistics
 * line and nowhere else, smaller than the median, muted where the median is ink, and below the
 * chart rather than above it. The four equal stat cards that used to sit here were removed for
 * exactly this reason — they made every number a headline, and one of them is now the mean.
 *
 * Accessibility, which is most of the work here
 * ---------------------------------------------
 * The bars are `aria-hidden` and a real `<table>` carries the same numbers, visually
 * hidden. That is not a courtesy: a bar chart conveys its content entirely through height
 * and position, both of which are unavailable to a screen reader, and a chart with an
 * `aria-label` saying "histogram of ratings" tells somebody nothing they can act on.
 *
 * Nothing is carried by colour alone. The reader's own answer is marked with the word "you"
 * above its bar and the bar filled solid rather than tinted; the median is marked with the word
 * "median" under its column and a rule. **The two sit on opposite sides of the axis**, which is
 * what lets both appear on one column — the commonest case on a small claim is that your own
 * answer is the median. Both survive being printed in grey, and both are in the table.
 *
 * Rendered empty and filled by script: the aggregate is deliberately not in the page source
 * until the reader has answered. See the header of src/lib/claims.ts.
 */
import { SCALE_ANCHORS, SCALE_POINTS } from '../lib/claims';
---

<div class="histogram" data-histogram hidden>
  {/* The plot is decoration over data the table below states properly. */}
  <div class="histogram__plot" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      SCALE_POINTS.map((point) => (
        <div class="histogram__col" data-col={point}>
          <span class="histogram__you" data-you hidden>you</span>
          <span class="histogram__count" data-count>0</span>
          <span class="histogram__bar" data-bar style="--height: 0%" />
          <span class="histogram__tick">{point}</span>
          <span class="histogram__anchor">{SCALE_ANCHORS[point] ?? ''}</span>
          <span class="histogram__median" data-median hidden>median</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <table class="visually-hidden" data-histogram-table>
    <caption>How people answered, by score</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Score</th>
        <th scope="col">People</th>
        <th scope="col">Notes</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody data-histogram-rows></tbody>
  </table>

  {/* Somebody who declined has no column, because "outside my expertise" is not a position on
      a scale of agreement — and parking them at 5 would file a declared non-opinion as a
      neutral opinion, which is the exact corruption the off-scale option exists to prevent. So
      they are said in words instead, right under the chart they are not in. Filled and shown by
      renderHistogram; absent when nobody declined. */}
  <p class="histogram__offscale" data-offscale hidden></p>

  {/* One line, not four cards.

      The cards were four equal boxes with the numbers set at --size-5, which made every one of
      them a headline — and one of them is now the mean. A headline mean is the specific thing
      the display rule forbids, because it is the reading that survives when somebody skims: on
      a bimodal claim it reports mild agreement for a community that has split cleanly in two.
      A line puts the mean where it belongs, beside the median, smaller than it, and downstream
      of the chart that contradicts it.

      Median first among the two summaries and set heavier, because it is the one that cannot
      invent a middle nobody occupies. Coverage is spelled as "N expressed an opinion" rather
      than as a percentage: a ratio invites reading it as a quality score for the claim, where
      the count invites the comparison that matters — how many of the people who answered were
      willing to put a number on it. */}
  <p class="histogram__line" data-histogram-line>
    <span class="histogram__figure"><b data-stat="positions">0</b> positions</span>
    <span class="histogram__figure"><b data-stat="opinions">0</b> expressed an opinion</span>
    <span class="histogram__figure histogram__figure--lead">median <b data-stat="median">—</b></span>
    <span class="histogram__figure histogram__figure--aside">mean <b data-stat="mean">—</b></span>
    <span class="histogram__figure" data-figure="contributions" hidden><b data-stat="contributions">0</b> contributions</span>
    <span class="histogram__figure" data-figure="changed" hidden><b data-stat="changed">0</b> changed position</span>
  </p>
</div>

<style>
  .histogram {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .histogram__plot {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(11, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    border-block-end: var(--border);
  }

  .histogram__col {
    /* No `position: relative` any more: it existed only to anchor the absolutely positioned
       dashed line that used to strike through this column's own numbers. */
    display: grid;
    /* you · count · bar · tick · anchor · median. Fixed rows so every column's tick sits on
       the same line however tall its bar is. */
    grid-template-rows: 1.5rem 1.25rem 10rem auto 2.4rem 1.4rem;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* Every child is placed explicitly, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. The "you"
     and "median" markers are `hidden` in most columns, and a hidden child is removed from
     the flow entirely — so with automatic placement the count would slide into row 1, the
     bar into row 2, and each column would be laid out differently depending on which
     markers it happened to carry. The result was eleven columns with their baselines at
     eleven different heights. */
  .histogram__you {
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .histogram__count {
    grid-row: 2;
  }

  .histogram__bar {
    grid-row: 3;
  }

  .histogram__tick {
    grid-row: 4;
  }

  .histogram__anchor {
    grid-row: 5;
  }

  .histogram__median {
    grid-row: 6;
  }

  .histogram__bar {
    align-self: end;
    width: 55%;
    height: var(--height);
    min-height: 2px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 42%, var(--surface));
  }

  /* ── Marking your answer, and marking the median ──────────────────────────────────
     These two collide constantly, because the commonest case on a small claim is that your
     own answer *is* the median — and the column that carries both is the one that used to carry
     a vertical dashed line straight through its own numbers.

     Three things went wrong there and all three are fixed by not overlaying anything:

       The dashed line was `position: absolute; left: 50%`, running from row 1 to the axis. The
       count and the tick are centred, so the line passed exactly through the middle of both
       digits and read as a strikethrough on them.

       The word "you" carried a `::before` diamond with `display: block`, which is two stacked
       lines of text in a row 1.5rem tall. It overflowed downwards onto the count.

       That diamond was U+25C6, which is outside the self-hosted `latin` and `latin-ext`
       subsets — so it was a fallback glyph beside IBM Plex, the same problem as U+25A0 in the
       tombstone and U+2192 in the movement badge. It is gone rather than redrawn: the word and
       the bar identify the column, and a glyph was never carrying meaning here.

     What marks them now, with nothing absolutely positioned and nothing overlapping:

       **Yours** — the word above the column, and the bar filled solid instead of tinted. Above
       the axis.
       **The median** — a rule and the word, below the axis.

     So the two live on opposite sides of the axis and can both appear on one column without
     touching. Neither is carried by colour alone: each has its word, and both are in the
     visually-hidden table regardless. */

  .histogram__col[data-mine] .histogram__bar {
    /* Solid where every other bar is 42% tinted. Not a colour *by position* — the accent is the
       same at 0 as at 10 — just the one bar the reader has a claim on. */
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .histogram__you,
  .histogram__median {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-0);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    /* One line, always. The row is 1.5rem and two lines do not fit in it — which is how the
       overflow onto the count happened in the first place. */
    white-space: nowrap;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .histogram__you {
    color: var(--accent-strong);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    /* Sat at the bottom of its row, so it reads as belonging to the column under it rather
       than floating above the whole chart. */
    align-self: end;
  }

  .histogram__median {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    border-block-start: var(--border-width-thick) solid var(--ink);
    color: var(--ink);
    align-self: start;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .histogram__count {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-0);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .histogram__tick {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
  }

  .histogram__anchor {
    font-size: var(--size-0);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* One line, wrapping on a narrow screen. The separator is a middot from ::before rather
     than a character in the markup, so the text equivalent above does not read a row of
     punctuation to somebody who is already getting these numbers from the table. */
  .histogram__line {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-2xs) 0;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--size-2);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .histogram__figure + .histogram__figure::before {
    content: '·';
    margin-inline: var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--rule);
  }

  /* Every number is mono, because monospace marks anything a machine could parse. */
  .histogram__figure b {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* The median leads. Heavier than the mean beside it and set in ink rather than muted,
     which is the whole of the visual hierarchy this line needs. */
  .histogram__figure--lead {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .histogram__figure--lead b {
    font-size: var(--size-3);
  }

  /* And the mean is the aside. Deliberately no larger than the surrounding prose: a mean set
     at display size is a headline whatever the words around it say. */
  .histogram__figure--aside b {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .histogram__offscale {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--size-2);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .histogram__frame {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  .histogram__why {
    font-size: var(--size-2);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .histogram__why summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--accent-strong);
  }

  .histogram__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
